# __NAME__

Dome resource-module scaffold for `__MODULE__`, pinned to Dome __VERSION__.
Linux amd64/arm64, Go 1.26.11+, Git and Task v3.45.4 are required. Docker is required
only for the explicitly requested container suites. The production Backend is
unfinished and fails explicitly; implement your host domain before using it.

```sh
task dev:setup
task check
task test:race
task test:integration
task test:e2e
task check:artifacts
go doc -all __MODULE__
go doc github.com/jani123/dome/core/resource
```

Import this library in your own agent and register `agent.WithModules(__NAME__.Module{Backend: yourBackend})`.
Construct resources in Go; decode configuration into typed structs. There is no
runtime plugin or automatic registration. Unit tests mock boundaries; real host
integration lives in tagged container suites. The file adapter under tests/ is
an educational fixture, not the production backend.

Read [authoring](docs/content/authoring.md), [development](docs/content/development.md),
[releases](docs/content/releases.md), [knowledge](knowledge/index.md), and
[OpenSpec requirements](openspec/specs/module-contract/spec.md).

Initialization expects an existing empty directory (including no `.git`). It
resolves dependencies and prepares developer assets before publication, never
runs an agent or Docker, initializes Git, or creates remote resources. Publication
into an existing directory has multiple entries and is not crash-atomic. A killed
initializer may leave partial output. Inspect it and remove only known generated
unchanged files, or start in a fresh empty directory; reruns refuse populated output.

Updates: generate a temporary repository using the target exact Dome version,
review dependency/skill/template changes and merge while preserving authored code.
Check the selected API with local `go doc`; public module retrieval may trigger
third-party indexing but no workflow requests or waits for pkg.go.dev.
